data: {"callId":"toolu_014kBCATmXLzzQRLszpBc2CX","name":"web_search","status":"completed","args":{"searchTerm":"Cursor IDE features","toolCallId":"toolu_014kBCATmXLzzQRLszpBc2CX"},"result":{"success":{"references":[{"title":"Web search results","chunk":"Links:\n1. [Cursor Agent](https://cursor.com/docs/agent/overview)\n2. [Cursor · Tab](https://cursor.com/tab)\n3. [Cursor Documentation](https://cursor.com/docs)\n4. [Prompting agents](https://cursor.com/docs/agent/prompting)\n5. [Agents window](https://cursor.com/docs/agent/agents-window)\n\nSynthesis:\nCursor is an AI coding IDE and agent that combines VS Code-style editing with AI assistance. Key features include Tab autocomplete with multi-line and cross-file suggestions, Cmd+K inline edits, and an Agent that can search code, edit files, run terminal commands, use the web, and create checkpoints. [1][2] It supports codebase-aware chat, @-context, images, documentation, rules, plugins, and multiple models. [3][4] It also offers Plan mode, Ask mode, CLI usage, parallel cloud agents, worktrees, and PR review workflows. [5][6]\n\nHighlights:\n<result id=\"1\">\n<title>Cursor Agent</title>\n<url>https://cursor.com/docs/agent/overview</url>\n<content>Agent is Cursor's assistant that can complete complex coding tasks independently, run terminal commands, and edit code. Access in sidepane with Cmd+I.\n...\n1. Instructions: The system prompt and rules that guide agent behavior\n2. Tools: File editing, codebase search, terminal execution, and more\n3. Model: The agent model you pick for the task\n...\nTools are the building blocks of Agent. They are used to search your codebase and the web to find relevant information, make edits to your files, run terminal commands, and more.\n...\nSearch for files by name, read directory structures, and find exact keywords or patterns within files.\n...\nGenerate search queries and perform web searches.\n...\nRetrieve specific rules based on type and description.\n...\nIntelligently read the content of a file. Also supports image files (.png, .jpg, .gif, .webp, .svg) and includes them in the conversation context for analysis by vision-capable models.\n...\nSuggest edits to files and apply them automatically.\n...\nExecute terminal commands and monitor output. By default, Cursor uses the first terminal profile available.\n...\nControl a browser to take screenshots, test applications, and verify visual changes. Agent can navigate pages, interact with elements, and capture the current state for analysis. See the Browser documentation for details.\n...\nclarifying questions during a task. While waiting ... continues reading files, making edits, or ... is incorporated as soon as it\n...\nYou have two ways to talk to an agent while it works. Queue a message and it waits for the current task to finish. Send a follow-up now and it steers the active turn at the agent's next tool call.\n...\nCLI, Ctrl ... pauses the goal. Pair a goal with ... Custom Mode when you want the agent to follow a playbook, or with the built-in `/loop` skill to run a prompt or skill on a recurring or variable interval. If you don't specify a fixed interval, the ... chooses when to ... . Stop the loop when you want it to end.</content>\n</result>\n\n<result id=\"2\">\n<title>Cursor · Tab</title>\n<url>https://cursor.com/tab</url>\n<content>Cursor · Tab\n\n# Next-action prediction\n\nTab anticipates where you're headed and suggests changes.\n\nThis element contains an interactive demo for sighted users. It's a demonstration of Cursor's IDE showing AI-powered coding assistance features. The interface is displayed over a scenic painted landscape wallpaper, giving the demo an artistic backdrop.\n...\n## Beyond autocomplete\n\nMulti-line changes, cross-file jumps, refactors that ripple through your codebase.\n\nInteractive demo with multiple windows showing Cursor's AI-powered features. The interface is displayed over a subtle, solid brand background.\n...\n## In-session context\n\nTab sees your current task, recent changes, and relevant files to make context-aware suggestions.\n\nInteractive demo with multiple windows showing Cursor's AI-powered features. The interface is displayed over a subtle, solid brand background.\n...\n## Proprietary model\n\nBuilt exclusively for Cursor, trained with RL on the largest sample of real-world scenarios.\n...\nA new interface and our first coding model, both purpose-built for working with agents.\n\nOur new Tab model makes 21% fewer suggestions while having 28% higher accept rate.\n\nAchieving a 3.5x MoE layer speedup with a complete rebuild for Blackwell GPUs.\n...\n## Beyond autocomplete\n\nMulti-line changes, cross-file jumps, refactors that ripple through your codebase.\n...\n## In-session context\n\nTab sees your current task, recent changes, and relevant files to make context-aware suggestions.\n...\n## Proprietary model\n\nBuilt exclusively for Cursor, trained with RL on the largest sample of real-world scenarios.</content>\n</result>\n\n<result id=\"3\">\n<title>Cursor Documentation</title>\n<url>https://cursor.com/docs</url>\n<content>Cursor is a coding agent for building ambitious software. Use it to understand your codebase, plan and build features, fix bugs, review changes, and work with the tools you already use.\n...\nStay up to date with the latest features and improvements\n\n## What you can do with Cursor\n\n### Understand your code\n\nTrace how a repo fits together and find the right places to start\n\n### Plan and build features\n\nScope changes, use Plan Mode, and ship bigger work with confidence\n\n### Find and fix bugs\n\nReproduce issues, narrow the root cause, and verify the fix\n\n### Review changes\n\nInspect diffs, run checks, and catch problems before you merge\n\n### Customize Cursor\n\nAdd plugins, skills, MCPs, and rules from one place\n\n### Connect your workflow\n\nWork with GitHub, GitLab, Azure DevOps, Bitbucket, JetBrains, Slack, Linear, and more\n...\n| Grok 4.7 ... k | 500k ... 0k; Fast mode is available ... 2x pricing; ... k) is billed at 3x standard rates |</content>\n</result>\n\n<result id=\"4\">\n<title>Prompting agents</title>\n<url>https://cursor.com/docs/agent/prompting</url>\n<content>Direct Agent with text prompts in the chat input. You can attach context, images, and voice, and switch models at any point.\n...\nType `@` in the chat input to attach specific context to your prompt. Start typing after `@` and Cursor shows matching suggestions.\n\n- Files & Folders: `@auth.ts` or `@src/components/` to include files or folders (type `/` after selecting a folder to navigate deeper)\n- Terminals: `@Terminals` to include terminal output as context\n- Chats: `@Chats` to reference context from a previous conversation\n- Git diffs: `@Commit (Diff of Working State)` for uncommitted changes, or `@Branch (Diff with Main)` for your full branch diff\n- Browser: `@Browser` to attach context from the built-in browser\n...\nType `/` in the chat input to invoke a skill. Pressing Enter attaches the skill to one message, and it fades as the conversation moves on. Use any skill as a Custom Mode to keep the agent focused on it while it works.\n...\nAttach images to your prompt to provide visual context for UI work, debugging, and design implementation.\n...\nClick the microphone icon in ... chat input to dictate your prompt instead of typing. Speak naturally, include technical details like file and function names, and review the transcription before sending.\n...\nEvery chat shares a fixed context window with the model. As you add files, run tools, and exchange messages, those tokens fill up. When the window gets close to full, Cursor compresses older parts of the conversation into a summary to leave more room for new conversation.\n...\nUse the model picker dropdown at the top of the chat input to switch models, or press Cmd / to cycle through models. The change applies to the current conversation going forward. Set a default model in Cursor Settings > Models.\n...\nfor quick edits\n...\nYou can switch models mid-conversation, for example when a faster model handled exploration but you need deeper reasoning for implementation. See Models & Pricing for the full list.</content>\n</result>\n\n<result id=\"5\">\n<title>Agents window</title>\n<url>https://cursor.com/docs/agent/agents-window</url>\n<content>The Agents Window is Cursor's agent-first interface. It provides a unified workspace to build with agents across repos and environments, including local, cloud, remote SSH, and more. It combines the power of parallel agents with the depth and control of a development environment.\n\nYou can switch back to the editor anytime, or have both open simultaneously.\n...\nIf you're in the editor, type Cmd+Shift+P → Open Agents Window to open the Agents Window.\n...\nTo return to the classic Cursor editor, type Cmd+Shift+P → Open Editor Window. This opens the current workspace in the editor.\n...\nIf you want to view or edit files without leaving the Agents Window, you can type Cmd+P to search files, or Cmd+Shift+F to search all files.\n...\n## Features Available Only in the Agents Window\n\nThe following features are available in the Agents Window:\n\n- **Multi-workspace:** work with agents across all your projects from one place.\n- **New diffs view:** review and commit changes, and manage PRs without leaving Cursor.\n- **Parallel agents:** run many parallel agents in the cloud (and work with them from your phone, web, Slack, GitHub, and Linear).\n- **Easier handoff between local and cloud:** quickly move an agent from cloud to local to iterate quickly, and move it back to the cloud so it keeps working on its own.\n- **Worktrees:** [run agents in isolated Git checkouts](https://cursor.com/docs/configuration/worktrees.md) so each task has its own files and changes.\n\n## Choosing Between Agents Window and Editor\n\nThe Agents Window works well when you want to run and manage many agents in parallel. If you are using agents to write most of your code, the Agents Window helps pull you up to a higher level of abstraction.\n\nThe editor works well when you want the classic IDE with VS Code extensions and flexible screen splitting to see many files at once.\n\nYou can move between the two interfaces, and we will continue to support and improve both experiences.</content>\n</result>"}]}}}

